[Printed proof step refuted: Appendix, p. 160] "Let $(G^1_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^1_{SU})$ be any compatible subgraph and trait set with the property that $\{i, u_1\} \in E^1_{SU}$ and $\{j, u_2\} \in E^1_{SU}$, where $u_1$ and $u_2$ are unsampled vertices with $Z^1_{u_1} = 0$ and $Z^1_{u_2} = 1$. Let $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU})$ be identical to $(G^1_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^1_{SU})$ except that the edges connecting $i$ and $j$ to $u_1$ and $u_2$ are swapped: $\{i, u_1\} 
otin E^2_{SU}$, $\{j, u_2\} 
otin E^2_{SU}$, and $\{i, u_2\} \in E^2_{SU}$ and $\{j, u_1\} \in E^2_{SU}$. Clearly $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU}) \in \mathrm{C}(G_R, \mathbf{d}_R, \mathbf{Z}_R)$." [Counterexample, from the validator] Observed data: $V_R = \{1, 2, 3\}$, $E_R = \{(1, 2), (2, 3)\}$, $M = \{1\}$, $(t_1, t_2, t_3) = (1, 2, 3)$, $\mathbf{d}_R = (3, 2, 3)$, $\mathbf{Z}_R = (0, 0, 1)$; so $(d_1^r, d_2^r, d_3^r) = (1, 2, 1)$. Take $i = 1$, $j = 3$: $d_1 > d_1^r$, $d_3 > d_3^r$, $Z_1 = 0 
eq 1 = Z_3$, so the hypotheses of Result 2 hold, with the normalization $Z_i = 0$, $Z_j = 1$ of the proof. Let $V^1_{SU} = \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\}$ with $u_1 = 4$, $u_2 = 5$ unsampled, $Z^1_4 = 0$, $Z^1_5 = 1$, and $E^1_{SU} = \{\{1, 2\}, \{2, 3\}, \{1, 4\}, \{3, 5\}, \{1, 5\}, \{3, 4\}\}$. The degrees are $(3, 2, 3, 2, 2)$; the pair satisfies conditions 1–4 of Definition 3 and contains $\{i, u_1\} = \{1, 4\}$ and $\{j, u_2\} = \{3, 5\}$: it is one of the starting pairs the proof allows. It also contains the cross edges $\{i, u_2\} = \{1, 5\}$ and $\{j, u_1\} = \{3, 4\}$. The pair described as $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU})$ ($\{1, 4\}$ and $\{3, 5\}$ absent, $\{1, 5\}$ and $\{3, 4\}$ present, all else the same) has $E^2_{SU} = \{\{1, 2\}, \{2, 3\}, \{1, 5\}, \{3, 4\}\}$ and degrees $(2, 2, 2, 1, 1)$: the sampled degrees are not $(3, 2, 3)$, condition 4 of Definition 3 fails, and $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU}) 
otin \mathrm{C}(G_R, \mathbf{d}_R, \mathbf{Z}_R)$, contrary to the printed "Clearly". The degrees and $|E_{SU}|$ differ between the two pairs, so the common denominator used in (A1) is not available either.

[Result 1, p. 155, unchanged] RESULT 1: Suppose there exist $i, j \in V_R$ with $i < j$, $(i, j) 
otin E_R$, $d_i^r < d_i$, and $d_j^r < d_j$. Then neither $G_S$ nor $G_{SU}$ are identified. [Proof, corrected: the starting graph is constructed, so that $\{i, j\}$ is absent from it (P012-E003), and the two recruitment-induced subgraphs are compatible in the sense defined at the opening of the proof (P012-N005)] Call a recruitment-induced subgraph $G_S = (V_S, E_S)$ compatible with the observed data if $V_S = V_R$, $\{i, j\} \in E_R$ implies $\{i, j\} \in E_S$, and $\sum_{j 
eq i} 1\{\{i, j\} \in E_S\} \leq d_i$ for each $i \in V_R$. Call an augmented recruitment-induced subgraph $G_{SU} = (V_{SU}, E_{SU})$ compatible with the observed data if conditions 1, 3, and 4 of definition 3 hold. Suppose $i \in V_R$ has $d_i^r < d_i$ and $j \in V_R$ has $d_j^r < d_j$, with $i < j$ and $(i, j) 
otin E_R$. Let $B = \{\{k, l\} : (k, l) \in E_R\}$ be the set of recruitment edges without their directions and $s_v = d_v - d_v^r \geq 0$ for $v \in V_R$; so $s_i \geq 1$, $s_j \geq 1$ and $\{i, j\} 
otin B$ (a recruitment edge runs from the earlier to the later vertex, and $(i, j) 
otin E_R$ with $i < j$). Let $G^1_{SU}$ be the graph whose vertices are those of $V_R$, one new unsampled vertex $u$, and further new unsampled vertices, $s_i - 1$ of them joined only to $i$, $s_j - 1$ joined only to $j$, and $s_v$ joined only to $v$ for every other $v \in V_R$; its edges are those of $B$, the edges $\{i, u\}$ and $\{j, u\}$, and the edges just described. Then: (a) $G^1_{SU}$ satisfies conditions 1, 3 and 4 of Definition 3 (with any binary traits on its unsampled vertices, $(G^1_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^1_{SU}) \in \mathrm{C}(G_R, \mathbf{d}_R, \mathbf{Z}_R)$), it contains $\{i, u\}$ and $\{j, u\}$, and $\{i, j\} 
otin E^1_{SU}$. Let $G^2_{SU}$ be obtained from $G^1_{SU}$ by removing the edges $\{i, u\}$, $\{j, u\}$ and the vertex $u$, and adding the edge $\{i, j\}$. Then: (b) $G^2_{SU}$ satisfies conditions 1, 3 and 4 of Definition 3, and $G^2_{SU} 
eq G^1_{SU}$, also up to renaming unsampled vertices ($\{i, j\}$ is an edge of $G^2_{SU}$ only). Because there exist at least 2 augmented recruitment-induced subgraphs compatible with the observed data, $G_{SU}$ is not identified. (c) Let $G^1_S$, $G^2_S$ be the recruitment-induced subgraphs obtained by removing the unsampled vertices (and the edges connected to them) from $G^1_{SU}$, $G^2_{SU}$. Their edge sets are $B$ and $B \cup \{\{i, j\}\}$. Both are compatible with the observed data in the sense defined at the opening of this proof, and $\{i, j\} 
otin G^1_S$, $\{i, j\} \in G^2_S$. Because there exist at least 2 recruitment-induced subgraphs compatible with the observed data, $G_S$ is not identified.

[Result 2, p. 155, unchanged] RESULT 2: Suppose there exist 2 vertices $i \in V_R$ and $j \in V_R$ such that $d_i > d_i^r$, $d_j > d_j^r$, and $Z_i 
eq Z_j$. Then $h$ is not point identified. [Proof, corrected: the starting pair is constructed, so that the cross edges $\{i, u_2\}$ and $\{j, u_1\}$ are absent from it (P012-E003); $h$ is written with its two arguments (P012-N006)] Suppose the observed RDS data are $G_R$, $\mathbf{Z}_R$, $\mathbf{t}_R$, $\mathbf{d}_R$, and there exist distinct $i \in V_R$ and $j \in V_R$ with $d_i^r < d_i$, $d_j^r < d_j$, and $Z_i 
eq Z_j$. Without loss of generality, suppose $Z_i = 0$ and $Z_j = 1$. Let $B = \{\{k, l\} : (k, l) \in E_R\}$ and $s_v = d_v - d_v^r \geq 0$ for $v \in V_R$. Let $G^1_{SU}$ be the graph whose vertices are those of $V_R$ and, for each $v \in V_R$, $s_v$ new unsampled vertices joined only to $v$, and whose edges are those of $B$ and the edges just described. Let $u_1$ be one of the new neighbours of $i$ and $u_2$ one of the new neighbours of $j$, and let $\mathbf{Z}^1_{SU}$ extend $\mathbf{Z}_R$ with $Z^1_{u_1} = 0$, $Z^1_{u_2} = 1$ and any binary traits on the other new vertices. Then: (a) $(G^1_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^1_{SU}) \in \mathrm{C}(G_R, \mathbf{d}_R, \mathbf{Z}_R)$, $\{i, u_1\} \in E^1_{SU}$, $\{j, u_2\} \in E^1_{SU}$, $\{i, u_2\} 
otin E^1_{SU}$ and $\{j, u_1\} 
otin E^1_{SU}$. Let $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU})$ be identical to $(G^1_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^1_{SU})$ except that $\{i, u_1\}$ and $\{j, u_2\}$ are removed and $\{i, u_2\}$ and $\{j, u_1\}$ are added. Then: (b) $(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU}) \in \mathrm{C}(G_R, \mathbf{d}_R, \mathbf{Z}_R)$, and the two pairs have the same vertices, the same degree at every vertex, the same number of edges $|E_{SU}|$ and the same traits. (c) With $Q = \sum_{k \in V_{SU}} d_k Z_k$, the denominator of (1) is the same for both pairs and equals $$D = \sum_{k,l \in V_{SU}} (d_k \delta_{kl} - d_k d_l/2|E_{SU}|) Z_k Z_l = Q -  rac{Q^2}{2|E_{SU}|} =  rac{Q\,(2|E_{SU}| - Q)}{2|E_{SU}|} > 0,$$ because $Q \geq d_j > 0$ and $2|E_{SU}| - Q \geq d_i > 0$. (d) [(A1)] With $h_1 = h(G^1_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^1_{SU})$ and $h_2 = h(G^2_{SU}, \mathbf{Z}^2_{SU})$, $$h_1 - h_2 =  rac{2\left(1 -  rac{d_j d_{u_2}}{2|E_{SU}|}ight) - 2\left(0 -  rac{d_j d_{u_2}}{2|E_{SU}|}ight)}{D} =  rac{2}{D} 
eq 0.$$ Therefore homophily is not point identified. [Exact instance, from the validator] $V_R = \{1, 2\}$, $E_R = \{(1, 2)\}$, $\mathbf{d}_R = (2, 2)$, $\mathbf{Z}_R = (0, 1)$, $i = 1$, $j = 2$, $u_1 = 3$, $u_2 = 4$ with traits $0, 1$: $E^1_{SU} = \{\{1, 2\}, \{1, 3\}, \{2, 4\}\}$, $E^2_{SU} = \{\{1, 2\}, \{1, 4\}, \{2, 3\}\}$, $|E_{SU}| = 3$, $Q = 3$, $D = 3/2$, $h_1 = 1/3$, $h_2 = -1$, $h_1 - h_2 = 4/3 = 2/D$.
